# <%= current_page.data.title %>

The Analytical Platform team's default position is to define a [development container](https://containers.dev/) (also known as dev container) in each repository.

> This guide presumes you are using VSCode.

## Launching a Development Container

1. Ensure Docker is running.
2. Install [Dev Containers - Visual Studio Marketplace](https://marketplace.visualstudio.com/items?itemName=ms-vscode-remote.remote-containers) on VSCode.
3. Clone a repository - in this example we will use [GitHub - ministryofjustice/analytical-platform](https://github.com/ministryofjustice/analytical-platform).
4. Open the repository on VSCode.
5. Open the **Command Palette** (`Ctrl+Shift+P` or `Cmd+Shift+P` on macOS).
   1. Select run the **"Dev Containers: Open Folder in Container..."**.
   2. If you have already built a dev container in this repository run the **"Dev Containers: Rebuild and Reopen in Container..."**.
6. The container should build, output from this process will appear in the terminal.
7. Upon completion you will be presented with a terminal.

## Predefined EKS Cluster Contexts

The development container in the [analytical-platform](https://github.com/ministryofjustice/analytical-platform) repository has the Analytical Platform EKS clusters predefined in the `~/.kube/config` file.

These can be consumed with the following commands:

List all available contexts with:

```shell
kubectl config get-contexts
```

Switch contexts:

```shell
kubectl config use-context <NAME>
```

See the current context with:

```shell
kubectl config current-context
```

